  "content": {
    "id": "p55-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "The polynomial $P(x)$ is divided by $(x+4)(x-3)$.\nFind the remainder if $P(-4)=11$ and $P(3)=-3$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$R(x)=3-2x$. Write $R(x)=ax+b$ and use the divisor's roots.",
      "worked": "The divisor has degree 2, so write the remainder as $ax+b$.\n$$P(x)=(x+4)(x-3)Q(x)+ax+b.$$\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-4)&=-4a+b=11,\\\\P(3)&=3a+b=-3.\\end{align*}$$\nSubtracting the first equation from the second:\n$$\\begin{align*}7a&=-14,\\\\a&=-2.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into $3a+b=-3$:\n$$\\begin{align*}3(-2)+b&=-3,\\\\b&=3,\\\\R(x)&=3-2x.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
